Osteogenesis Imperfecta (Acute Musculoskeletal Complication Risk Assessment)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Multiple low-trauma fractures, Blue sclerae, Dentinogenesis imperfecta

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-038
Reason for Visit: Sudden, severe, localized arm pain and deformity following minimal trauma in a patient with a severe, known congenital connective tissue disorder.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a pediatric patient with known Osteogenesis Imperfecta who sustained a minor fall and now presents with severe arm pain and visible deformity. Urgent clinical assessment was strictly mandated to confirm an acute fragility fracture, rapidly evaluate for neurovascular compromise, and initiate immediate, exceptionally gentle stabilization and potent analgesia prior to emergency transport or daytime specialized orthopedic review.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "He just tripped on the rug, put his arm out, and we heard a snap; he's screaming in pain and his forearm looks bent."
History of Present Illness: A 10-year-old patient with a known, genetically confirmed diagnosis of Osteogenesis Imperfecta presents with an acute, severe right forearm injury. Timeline: Ten minutes prior to the call, the patient tripped on a living room rug and fell from a standing height, landing on an outstretched right hand. Patient immediately experienced agonizing, sharp, localized pain in the mid-forearm. The parent reports hearing a distinct "snap." The patient is in extreme distress, completely refusing to move the arm, and supporting it against their chest. The parent notes this is consistent with previous fragility fractures the child has suffered.
Functional Status: Acutely distressed; right upper limb immobilized by pain and visible deformity.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety environment; parent is highly experienced with the condition but distressed by the acute fracture event.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 115 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 28 Kg
General: Alert, crying, in extreme distress, cradling the right arm securely against the torso.
Physical Exam: Physical features of Osteogenesis Imperfecta are clinically evident: distinctive blue/grey discoloration of the sclerae, short stature disproportionate to parental genetics, and dentinogenesis imperfecta (discolored, opalescent teeth). Inspection of the right forearm reveals a visible, angular deformity in the mid-shaft (classic "dinner fork" or bowing appearance indicative of fracture). Exquisite, severe bony point tenderness is present over the deformity. No open skin wounds. Crucially, neurological and vascular examination distal to the injury is intact: radial and ulnar pulses are palpable and strong, capillary refill is brisk (<2 sec), and sensation to light touch is preserved in all digits. The patient can actively wiggle all fingers, confirming major motor nerve continuity.
Environmental Observations: Rug edge identified as the mechanism of the low-trauma fall.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications administered prior to arrival.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Suspected Acute Fragility Fracture of the Right Forearm (Radius/Ulna) secondary to Osteogenesis Imperfecta - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Acute Compartment Syndrome - Ruled out currently by the presence of strong distal pulses, brisk capillary refill, soft forearm compartments, and lack of pain out of proportion to the visible fracture, but requires continuous monitoring.

Radial Head Subluxation (Nursemaid's Elbow) - Ruled out by the distinct mid-shaft bony deformity, the mechanism of a fall (compression) rather than longitudinal traction, and exquisite mid-shaft point tenderness.

Soft Tissue Contusion - Ruled out definitively by the gross anatomical deformity and the classic snapping mechanism in a highly susceptible patient.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The pediatric patient, highly vulnerable due to profound congenital bone fragility, presented with classic signs of an acute low-trauma fracture (deformity, point tenderness, mechanism). Given the underlying Osteogenesis Imperfecta, the bones are exceptionally brittle and susceptible to displacement. Delaying clinical assessment or attempting unguided movement risked severe neurovascular compromise, unacceptable suffering, and worsening of the deformity. Urgent after-hours assessment safely confirmed the clinical diagnosis, established that distal neurovascular status was currently intact, and allowed for the immediate, highly specialized (gentle) provisional splinting and administration of potent parenteral analgesia to stabilize the child for urgent, specialized orthopedic transport.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Intranasal Fentanyl (weight-based dose) STAT for rapid, needle-free, potent analgesia. Carefully applied a well-padded, lightweight SAM splint to the right forearm, immobilizing the wrist and elbow joints without applying constrictive pressure.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Keep the splinted arm resting on a soft pillow across the chest, remaining completely still while waiting for transport. Consequence: Prevention of the broken bone ends grinding against each other, immediately halting the sharp, terrifying spikes of pain (negative reinforcement).
Action: Take slow, deep "balloon breaths" while the medicine takes effect. Consequence: Reduces the physical panic response, allowing the strong painkillers to work more effectively and calming the overall nervous system (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Immediately arranged for non-emergency patient transport (or guided parental transport if deemed safe and faster) to the designated pediatric orthopedic center.
DME/Safety (Optional): Applied a padded SAM splint and broad arm sling.
Follow-up: Urgent pediatric orthopedic surgery review for specialized imaging and definitive management (potentially requiring specialized rodding techniques due to poor bone stock).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation (or dial 000 during transport) if the child's fingers become suddenly cold, pale, or blue, if they lose the ability to wiggle their fingers, or if the pain suddenly becomes unmanageable despite the fentanyl.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis of a fragility fracture is clinically certain given the deformity and history. Specialized pediatric orthopedic care is required to manage the fracture in the context of the brittle bone disease. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: (Handover provided to parents and transport team).

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor the fingers constantly. If they turn pale or blue, or if he cannot feel you touching them, the blood supply may be pinched, and we must call 000 immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The fall has caused one or both of the brittle bones in the forearm to break. The strong pain medicine given through the nose works very quickly to dull the sharp pain. The padded splint acts as a temporary cast to keep the bone perfectly still, protecting the nerves and blood vessels until the specialist doctors can take X-rays.
Actionable Steps: Keep the arm perfectly still on the pillow. Do not try to adjust the splint. Focus on slow, calm breathing. We are ready to transport you to the hospital now.

